---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ArticleSchema from "../../components/schema/ArticleSchema.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import FrameworkBadge from "../../components/FrameworkBadge.svelte";

const tableOfContents = [
  { id: "overview", label: "Overview" },
  { id: "reframe", label: "Not competitors" },
  { id: "overlap-math", label: "The overlap math" },
  { id: "fully-covered", label: "What SOC 2 already covers" },
  { id: "reformat-layer", label: "Partial overlap" },
  { id: "net-new", label: "The 8% net-new" },
  { id: "sequencing", label: "Sequencing" },
  { id: "burn-money", label: "Where money is wasted" },
];
---

<Layout
  title={"ISO 27001 After SOC 2: The 30% Shortcut"}
  description={"If you have SOC 2 Type 2, you're 70% of the way to ISO 27001. See what to reuse vs reformat vs build new, and how to avoid paying twice for the same controls."}
  ogImage="/hub/ISO27001-og.png"
>
  <ArticleSchema
    title="ISO 27001 for Companies That Already Have SOC 2: The 30% Shortcut"
    description="If you have SOC 2 Type 2, you are roughly 70% of the way to ISO 27001. Learn what is reuse vs reformat vs net-new, how to sequence the project, and how to avoid paying for the same controls twice."
    url="https://www.probo.com/hub/iso-27001-after-soc-2-the-30-percent-shortcut"
    image="https://www.probo.com/hub/ISO27001-og.png"
    datePublished="2026-04-23"
    dateModified="2026-08-31"
  />
  <main class="container my-10 sm:my-20">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[
        { label: "Hub", href: "/hub" },
        { label: "ISO 27001 after SOC 2" },
      ]}
      class="mb-6"
    />
    <header class="mb-12 text-center sm:mb-16">
      <div
        class="mb-4 flex flex-col items-center justify-center gap-4 sm:flex-row"
      >
        <h1
          class="text-foreground max-w-[920px] text-[2rem] leading-[1.12] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] sm:text-[2.75rem]"
        >
          {"ISO 27001 for Companies That Already Have SOC 2:"}
          <span class="highlight-text">{"The 30% Shortcut"}</span>
        </h1>
        <div class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-2">
          <FrameworkBadge name="SOC2_TYPE2" class="size-12" client:visible />
          <FrameworkBadge name="ISO27001" class="size-12" client:visible />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div
        class="mb-6 inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-gray-200 bg-white/80 px-5 py-2.5 text-base text-gray-500 shadow-sm backdrop-blur-sm"
      >
        {"From SOC 2 evidence to ISO certification"}
      </div>
      <p
        class="text-muted-foreground mx-auto max-w-[800px] text-lg leading-relaxed"
      >
        {
          "Most ISO 27001 quotes assume a greenfield build. If your SOC 2 Type 2 is real, the work is mostly overlap, and this guide maps the 70% you already have, the 22% to reformat, and the 8% you must build."
        }
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-10 lg:flex-row lg:gap-16">
      <aside class="shrink-0 lg:w-56">
        <nav class="lg:sticky lg:top-24">
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
            {
              tableOfContents.map((item) => (
                <button
                  type="button"
                  data-section={item.id}
                  class="toc-link cursor-pointer border-none bg-transparent p-0 text-left text-sm leading-5 text-[#6b716a] transition-colors hover:text-[#141e12]"
                >
                  {item.label}
                </button>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </nav>
      </aside>

      <article class="max-w-[720px] flex-1">
        <section id="overview" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            {"When SOC 2 is not enough for your European buyer"}
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              {
                `Your biggest European prospect just replied to your SOC 2 report with one line: "Thanks, but we'll need ISO 27001 before we can sign."`
              }
            </p>
            <p>
              {
                `You forward it to your CTO. The response is predictable. "Didn't we just spend six months on compliance?"`
              }
            </p>
            <p>
              {
                "Yes. You did. And the good news your CTO doesn't know yet: you're already "
              }
              <strong class="text-foreground"
                >{"roughly 70% of the way to ISO 27001"}</strong
              >{
                ". You just can't see it, because nobody has mapped the two frameworks against each other in a way that's actually useful to a founder."
              }
            </p>
            <p>
              {
                "Most vendors will quote you an ISO 27001 project like it's a greenfield build: six months, $40–60k, a fresh Statement of Applicability, a new auditor, a new policy stack. That's the lazy version. It's also how compliance platforms make their money twice."
              }
            </p>
            <p>
              {
                "The real shape of the work is different. If you have a clean SOC 2 Type 2 in hand, "
              }
              <strong class="text-foreground"
                >{"ISO 27001 is a 30% shortcut, not a 100% restart."}</strong
              >
              {
                " This article tells you exactly which 30% is net-new, which 70% is already done, and how to sequence the project so you don't pay for the same controls twice."
              }
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <section id="reframe" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            {"The reframe: SOC 2 and ISO 27001 are not competitors"}
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              {
                `They're overlapping specs for the same underlying thing: "prove you're not reckless with information."`
              }
            </p>
            <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><a href="/hub/soc2" class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                    ><strong class="text-foreground">{"SOC 2"}</strong></a
                  >{
                    " is an American attestation standard. Five Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy). An auditor reviews your controls and writes a report. The output is a PDF your sales team sends to buyers."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><a
                    href="/hub/iso27001"
                    class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                    ><strong class="text-foreground">{"ISO 27001"}</strong></a
                  >{
                    " is an international certification standard. One control set (Annex A, 93 controls in the 2022 revision) wrapped around a formal management system called an ISMS. An auditor assesses you and issues a certificate. The output is a one-page cert your sales team sends to buyers, usually European ones."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              {"Different outputs. Different auditors. Different vocabulary. "}
              <strong class="text-foreground"
                >{"Deeply overlapping evidence."}</strong
              >
            </p>
            <p>
              {
                'The reason nobody frames it this way is that the two standards use different words for the same thing. SOC 2 talks about "control objectives." ISO talks about "Annex A controls." SOC 2 has a "System Description." ISO has a "Statement of Applicability." Underneath the jargon, you\'re proving the same things: you manage access, you encrypt data, you respond to incidents, you screen your vendors, you train your people.'
              }
            </p>
            <p>
              {
                "If your SOC 2 is real (not a paper exercise), you have already built most of an ISMS without calling it that."
              }
            </p>
            <p>
              {"Still choosing a first framework? Start with "}
              <a
                href="/hub/which-compliance-framework"
                class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                >{"which compliance framework you need"}</a
              >
              {" and the head-to-head on "}
              <a
                href="/blog/2025-10-08-soc2-or-iso27001"
                class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline">{"SOC 2 vs ISO 27001"}</a
              >
              {"."}
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <section id="overlap-math" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            {"The overlap math"}
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              {
                `Here's where the "30% shortcut" number comes from. The 2022 version of ISO 27001 Annex A has `
              }
              <strong class="text-foreground">{"93 controls"}</strong>
              {
                " across four themes: Organizational (37), People (8), Physical (14), Technological (34)."
              }
            </p>
            <p>{"Mapped against a well-run SOC 2 Type 2 scope:"}</p>
            <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"~65 controls (≈70%)"}</strong
                  >{
                    " are fully or substantially covered by SOC 2 evidence you already have. Access management, change management, cryptography, logging, vendor risk, incident response, acceptable use, background checks, and all of it is in your existing SOC 2 scope."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"~20 controls (≈22%)"}</strong
                  >{
                    " are partial overlaps. You have the control, but ISO wants it documented differently or applied to a broader scope. Example: SOC 2 wants a risk assessment; ISO wants a documented risk treatment plan with owners and review cycles."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground">{"~8 controls (≈8%)"}</strong
                  >{
                    " are genuinely net-new. The ISMS itself, the Statement of Applicability, internal audits of the ISMS, the management review meeting, and a handful of Annex A items that SOC 2 doesn't map to at all (like threat intelligence and information security during disruption)."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-foreground"
                >{"That's the shape of the work."}</strong
              >
              {" ~70% reuse. ~22% reformat. ~8% build from scratch."}
            </p>
            <p>
              {
                "The 6-month, $40k ISO project most vendors quote is priced like all 93 controls are new. They're not. You've already paid for two-thirds of them."
              }
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <section id="fully-covered" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            {"What's fully covered by your existing SOC 2"}
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              {
                "If you have a SOC 2 Type 2 report covering the Security Trust Services Criterion, the following Annex A controls are essentially free:"
              }
            </p>
            <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Access controls (A.5.15–A.5.18, A.8.2–A.8.5)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " Your SOC 2 already tested user provisioning, deprovisioning, MFA, privileged access, and quarterly access reviews. Same evidence, new label."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Cryptography (A.8.24)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " You're already encrypting data at rest and in transit. Same KMS, same TLS config, same evidence screenshots."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Change management (A.8.32)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " Your SOC 2 tested your PR review and deployment process. ISO wants the same thing."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Logging and monitoring (A.8.15–A.8.17)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " Your SOC 2 auditor already reviewed your log retention, alerting, and clock sync. Hand them the same exports."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Vendor management (A.5.19–A.5.23)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " Your SOC 2 vendor inventory, risk ratings, and contract reviews map one-to-one."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Incident response (A.5.24–A.5.27)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " Same playbook, same tabletop exercise evidence, same post-mortems."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"HR security (A.6.1–A.6.8)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " Background checks, onboarding, offboarding, security awareness training. All already in your SOC 2 scope."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Physical security (A.7.1–A.7.14)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " If you're cloud-native, you inherit most of these from your AWS/GCP/Azure SOC 2 / ISO 27001 reports. Same inheritance, same sub-service organization letter."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{
                      "Backup, availability, capacity (A.8.13–A.8.14, A.8.6)."
                    }</strong
                  >{
                    " If your SOC 2 scope included Availability, these are done. If not, you still have most of the evidence, but it was just out of scope."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              {
                "That's roughly 65 of the 93 controls. Your SOC 2 auditor's workpapers are the ISO auditor's evidence. You just re-package them under the Annex A labels."
              }
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <section id="reformat-layer" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            {`What's a partial overlap, the "reformat" layer`}
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              {
                "This is where ISO quietly adds scope. The control exists in your SOC 2 world; ISO wants it documented with more structure."
              }
            </p>
            <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{
                      "Risk assessment and treatment (A.5.1–A.5.3, Clause 6)."
                    }</strong
                  >{
                    " SOC 2 asks you to do a risk assessment. ISO asks you to do a risk assessment "
                  }<em>{"plus"}</em>{
                    " a formal risk treatment plan, with named owners, residual risk ratings, and annual review. Your SOC 2 risk register probably needs three new columns and a sign-off cycle."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Asset inventory (A.5.9–A.5.14)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " SOC 2 wants a list of systems. ISO wants a classified inventory, with owners, data classification, and acceptable use tied to each asset. Usually a spreadsheet upgrade, not a new control."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Supplier security (A.5.19–A.5.23)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " SOC 2 covers vendor reviews. ISO adds explicit requirements around supplier agreements, monitoring, and information security in ICT supply chains. Most founders need to upgrade their DPA/MSA templates once."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Information classification (A.5.12)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " SOC 2 tends to leave this implicit. ISO wants a classification scheme (Public / Internal / Confidential / Restricted) and evidence that people apply it. Light lift: a policy plus a labeling convention."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Secure development (A.8.25–A.8.31)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " SOC 2 covers SDLC. ISO adds explicit requirements around threat modeling, secure coding guidelines, and separation of dev/test/prod. Most engineering-led startups already do this; they've just never written it down."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              {
                "None of this is hard. It's documentation polish on controls you already operate. Budget "
              }
              <strong class="text-foreground"
                >{"40–60 hours of policy-and-evidence rework"}</strong
              >{", not a quarter-long project."}
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <section id="net-new" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            {`What's genuinely net-new, the 8% you actually have to build`}
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              {
                "This is the real work. None of it is hard on its own. All of it is invisible to a SOC 2 auditor, so none of it exists in your current setup."
              }
            </p>
            <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"The ISMS itself (Clauses 4–10)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " ISO is not just a control list; it's a management system. You need a documented scope, an information security policy, objectives with measurable targets, and a governance rhythm that actually happens. This is the single biggest conceptual leap from SOC 2."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Statement of Applicability (Clause 6.1.3 / A)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " A document that lists all 93 Annex A controls, says which apply to you, which don't, and why. It's the spine of the audit. Budget half a day to draft, then iterate."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Internal audit program (Clause 9.2)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " ISO requires you to audit your own ISMS at least annually, using someone independent of the function being audited. For a 20-person startup, this usually means a fractional resource or your compliance partner. SOC 2 has no equivalent requirement."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Management review (Clause 9.3)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " A formal, documented meeting where leadership reviews ISMS performance: incidents, audit findings, risk posture, objective tracking. Quarterly works. No meeting, no certificate."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Continual improvement (Clause 10)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " Documented corrective actions with root cause analysis for every nonconformity. More structured than a SOC 2 finding log."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{"Threat intelligence (A.5.7, new in 2022)."}</strong
                  >{
                    " You need a documented process for collecting and acting on threat intel. For most SaaS startups, this is a lightweight feed (CISA alerts, vendor advisories, your cloud provider's security bulletins) with a review cadence."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{
                      "Information security for use of cloud services (A.5.23, new in 2022)."
                    }</strong
                  >{
                    " Most of this inherits from your existing vendor reviews, but ISO wants explicit cloud-service selection and exit criteria documented."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground"
                    >{
                      "ICT readiness for business continuity (A.5.30, new in 2022)."
                    }</strong
                  >{
                    " Adjacent to your SOC 2 BCP, but ISO is more explicit. You need documented RTOs/RPOs tied to business impact."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-foreground"
                >{
                  "This is the 8% that actually changes your operating model."
                }</strong
              >
              {" Everything else is re-labeling."}
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <section id="sequencing" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            {"The sequencing that saves you money"}
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              {
                "Most founders treat ISO 27001 as a separate project from their next SOC 2 cycle. That's the expensive way. For the greenfield number, see "
              }
              <a
                href="/hub/iso27001-certification-cost"
                class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                >{"ISO 27001 certification cost"}</a
              >
              {". The cheap way:"}
            </p>
            <ol class="ml-0 list-none space-y-4">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="text-foreground font-semibold">1.</span>
                <span
                  >{
                    "Align your SOC 2 renewal and your ISO 27001 stage 1 so they share controls, evidence, and policy reviews. One evidence-collection sprint, two certificates."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="text-foreground font-semibold">2.</span>
                <span
                  >{
                    "Pick an auditor who does both. Many SOC 2 firms also issue ISO certs via an accredited partner (UKAS, ANAB, etc.). One kickoff, one stakeholder burden, lower combined fee. A standalone ISO auditor who's never seen your SOC 2 will ask for the same evidence three different ways."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="text-foreground font-semibold">3.</span>
                <span
                  >{
                    "Reuse the policy stack, don't duplicate it. One information security policy that satisfies both frameworks is allowed and encouraged. Two parallel policy libraries is how compliance becomes a full-time job for someone on your team."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="text-foreground font-semibold">4.</span>
                <span
                  >{
                    "Build the ISMS before the audit gap. The 8% of net-new controls need to run for "
                  }<strong class="text-foreground">{"at least 3 months"}</strong
                  >{
                    " before your ISO stage 2 audit. Management review minutes from last week won't fly. Start the ISMS rhythm the day you decide to pursue ISO, not the month before the audit."
                  }</span
                >
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="text-foreground font-semibold">5.</span>
                <span
                  >{
